1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:09,902
This is the oracle, your personal research memory in Murmurent. It remembers what you found, and gives it back months later with its source.

2
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:21,895
Its own file says what it is for: to accumulate, organize and recall scientific knowledge across all of your projects. There is one oracle per person, not one per project.

3
00:00:21,895 --> 00:00:34,533
Every entry lands in your own Obsidian vault, where you can browse, search and graph it. Sharing a finding with the lab oracle is a separate step that you take yourself.

4
00:00:34,949 --> 00:00:45,152
What it will not do is just as clear. It does not publish to the lab; it only stages a draft. It never publishes a protected or restricted entry.

5
00:00:45,152 --> 00:00:57,512
It does not fetch literature or run analyses; the bookworm and the blacksmith do that. And it never fabricates: if it holds nothing on a topic, it says so.

6
00:00:57,899 --> 00:01:08,055
A real run, on a demo vault. The member asks it to remember a finding from a synthetic colony study, with the project, the experiment and their handle.

7
00:01:08,055 --> 00:01:17,832
The oracle writes one entry with the full schema, and adds a line to the index. The first line of its reply is the verdict.

8
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:29,652
Asked later what it knows about serum and colony size, it finds that one entry, cites the project and the experiment, and says plainly that it holds nothing else.

9
00:01:30,049 --> 00:01:43,038
To use it, type to Claude Code and name the agent: ask the oracle to remember something, or ask it what you know about a topic. Give it the finding, where it came from, and the sensitivity.

10
00:01:43,038 --> 00:01:57,645
The first line of the reply is the verdict: Found, Not found or Unsure, or Remembered when it filed something. The detail names the entry, so you can open it in Obsidian.
